     1	"""Per-instance salt for hash identifier privacy (ADR-079).
     2	
     3	Provides ``get_instance_salt() -> bytes`` returning a 32-byte salt
     4	loaded from ``~/.claude/projects/<slug>/.salt``. Generates the salt
     5	on first call (``os.urandom(32)`` + file mode ``0o600``).
     6	
     7	PLAN-058 Round-23 (2026-04-24) — closes the REAL identifier-privacy
     8	issue at ``UserPromptSubmit.py:182`` (``prompt_sha`` is unsalted
     9	SHA-256 of the user prompt; correlation oracle for any party with
    10	audit-log read access). The Phase B audit's F-SEC-03 finding
    11	referenced a phantom ``_hash_injection_prefix`` that does not exist;
    12	the real attack surface is ``prompt_sha256`` published by every
    13	``prompt_submitted`` audit event. See ADR-079 §Phantom rejection
    14	for the full forensic record.
    15	
    16	## Fail-open contract (ADR-005, ADR-010)
    17	
    18	On any I/O failure (permission denied, disk full, broken symlink)
    19	the function returns ``b""`` instead of raising. Callers compose the
    20	empty salt with their input — the resulting hash degrades to the
    21	pre-fix unsalted form. Confidentiality is best-effort; availability
    22	is invariant.
    23	
    24	## No rotation
    25	
    26	Salt is generated once per installation and never rotated. Rotating
    27	the salt would invalidate ``prompt_sha256`` correlations across all
    28	historical audit events (the chief use of the field). Per-instance
    29	salt provides the desired property — cross-instance correlation is
    30	impossible for an external observer — without sacrificing single-
    31	instance forensic correlation across time.
    32	
    33	## Thread safety
    34	
    35	The module-level ``_CACHED_SALT`` is read-once-write-once. Concurrent
    36	readers in the unloaded state may both invoke ``os.urandom`` + write,
    37	but the second writer's ``write_bytes`` is atomic on POSIX (single
    38	``write(2)`` for 32 bytes). The losing writer's bytes are discarded;
    39	the winner's bytes seed the cache for both processes on next call.
    40	This is acceptable because hooks run as short-lived subprocesses;
    41	the race window is sub-millisecond and the salt remains 32 random
    42	bytes either way.
    43	
    44	## Stdlib-only
    45	
    46	Per hook discipline (ADR-002): ``os`` + ``pathlib`` only.
    47	"""
    48	from __future__ import annotations
    49	
    50	import os
    51	from pathlib import Path
    52	from typing import Optional
    53	
    54	
    55	_SALT_FILENAME = ".salt"
    56	_SALT_BYTES = 32
    57	_SALT_MODE = 0o600
    58	_DIR_MODE = 0o700
