     1	"""Per-instance salt for hash identifier privacy (ADR-079).
     2	
     3	Provides ``get_instance_salt() -> bytes`` returning a 32-byte salt
     4	loaded from ``~/.claude/projects/<slug>/.salt``. Generates the salt
     5	on first call (``os.urandom(32)`` + file mode ``0o600``).
     6	
     7	PLAN-058 Round-23 (2026-04-24) — closes the REAL identifier-privacy
     8	issue at ``UserPromptSubmit.py:182`` (``prompt_sha`` is unsalted
     9	SHA-256 of the user prompt; correlation oracle for any party with
    10	audit-log read access). The Phase B audit's F-SEC-03 finding
    11	referenced a phantom ``_hash_injection_prefix`` that does not exist;
    12	the real attack surface is ``prompt_sha256`` published by every
    13	``prompt_submitted`` audit event. See ADR-079 §Phantom rejection
    14	for the full forensic record.
    15	
    16	## Fail-open contract (ADR-005, ADR-010)
    17	
    18	On any I/O failure (permission denied, disk full, broken symlink)
    19	the function returns ``b""`` instead of raising. Callers compose the
    20	empty salt with their input — the resulting hash degrades to the
    21	pre-fix unsalted form. Confidentiality is best-effort; availability
    22	is invariant.
    23	
    24	## No rotation — with exactly ONE sanctioned, REGISTERED exception
    25	
    26	Salt is generated once per PROJECT (ADR-079 S318 amendment: the salt
    27	unit is the project, resolved via ``_lib/runtime_paths`` — the
    28	per-``$HOME`` reading made ``prompt_sha256`` correlate ACROSS projects,
    29	the exact oracle this module exists to close) and never rotated.
    30	Rotating the salt would invalidate ``prompt_sha256`` correlations
    31	across all historical audit events (the chief use of the field).
    32	
    33	The one exception is the PLAN-182 migration itself: the project that
    34	inherits the historical chain (W2 custody decision) inherits the
    35	legacy ``.salt`` byte-for-byte; every OTHER project mints fresh — and
    36	that minting is OBSERVABLE, never silent (the pre-S318 code minted
    37	with no error, no log, no signal — silent rotation against this very
    38	section). Every mint now (a) writes a ``salt-minted.json`` marker
    39	sidecar next to the salt (forensic ground truth) and (b) best-effort
    40	emits the registered ``salt_rotation_registered`` chain event.
    41	
    42	## Thread safety
    43	
    44	The module-level ``_CACHED_SALT`` is read-once-write-once, and the
    45	FIRST MINT is elected by the filesystem: ``_generate_and_persist``
    46	creates with ``O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW``, so exactly one process
    47	wins and every loser RE-READS the winner's bytes.
    48	
    49	rc.1 re-pass part 6 C2 — this section used to accept the race, on the
    50	grounds that "the winner's bytes seed the cache for both processes on
    51	next call". That was false: the cache is a hit before any disk read
    52	(``get_instance_salt`` returns at the ``_CACHED_SALT`` check), so each
    53	loser kept and used the value it had generated, and the ``.salt`` on
    54	disk was whatever the last ``write_bytes`` left behind. The re-pass
    55	reproduced it with a spin barrier: **5 of 5 rounds, 6 of 6 processes
    56	holding DISTINCT salts, 5 of them orphaned** — and every
    57	``prompt_sha256`` an orphan emitted is irreproducible from the
    58	persisted salt, which is the one thing this module exists to keep
    59	reproducible. The window is small and opens at most once per project,
    60	but the upgrade to v1.4 re-opens it in EVERY project at once.
    61	
    62	A salt that is already on disk and MALFORMED is a different case: that
    63	is a deliberate rotation (``reason="other"``), and it still replaces
    64	the file — with ``O_NOFOLLOW`` but without ``O_EXCL``.
    65	
    66	## Stdlib-only
    67	
    68	Per hook discipline (ADR-002): stdlib only. The only intra-``_lib``
    69	import is ``runtime_paths`` (itself a stdlib-only leaf), so the module
    70	stays loadable from any hook; ``audit_emit`` is imported LAZILY inside
    71	the mint branch only (mint happens at most once per project lifetime).
    72	"""
